83: ### Measured cost and required rerun
84: 
85: The 15 completed exact-physics result receipts report:
86: 
87: - wall time per anchor: minimum 397.652 s, mean 438.728 s, maximum 490.400 s;
88: - boundary evaluations per anchor: minimum 3,468, mean 3,534.667, maximum 3,588;
89: - measured generation peak RSS: 1,930,498,048 bytes;
90: - runner SHA before/after identical for all 15 receipts, with `fallback_source_value_restored=true` for all 15.
91: 
92: These are measured costs of the current default-plus-singletons exact physics pass, not a measurement of the unperformed joint extension. The latter cannot honestly be assigned a measured incremental time from the available receipts.
93: 
94: To make C3 possible, physics must be rerun per anchor with a declared coalition support. For every emitted changed set `A`, that rerun must:
95: 
96: 1. construct the genuine configuration whose mapping differs from that anchor's `default_mapping` on exactly all users in `A`;
97: 2. submit the default, every member's unilateral configuration, and the joint configuration through the same `StepEvaluator.evaluate_many` path, transition/incumbent, rekey set, nominal field, boundary set, calibration, and physics source;
98: 3. persist the joint configuration's `current`, `phi_current`, `projections`, and `delta_from_default`, alongside its singleton dependencies;
99: 4. derive `Delta F(A)`, every `d_i`, and `Psi_A` from those persisted real outcomes; and
100: 5. rebuild C1, C2, and C3 views over one resulting physics digest.
101: 
102: The existing pilot code shows the correct kind of joint path: it evaluates a joint catalogue at lines 761–809 and passes the reference, unilateral, and selected joint outcomes at lines 932–977: [run_v025_pilot_c3.py joint evaluation](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:761) and [run_v025_pilot_c3.py outcome assembly](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:932). That source was read only; it was not edited or executed for physics here.
103: 
104: Because the current physics files and their sidecars are authenticated/sealed, adding joint records in place would alter sealed artefacts. A new physics corpus (or an explicitly authorized full replacement) is required; a view-only operation is insufficient.
105: 
106: ## Derived on paper
107: 
108: For a singleton `A = {i}` using one common evaluation path,
109: 
110: `Psi_{ {i} } = Delta F({i}) - d_i = 0`
111: 
112: exactly by definition. This is an algebraic identity, not a floating-point tolerance claim. The reader's trainable C3 schema requires `|A| >= 2`, so no singleton C3 rows were expected or emitted. Since no support was emitted, the requested emitted-support singleton-zero test has no observations and is reported as not applicable—not as a vacuous pass.
113: 
114: Likewise, the decomposition identity for a valid future row is `C1(A) + Psi_A = Delta F(A)`. It cannot be evaluated for the absent multi-user outcomes. No residual is reported or fabricated.
115: 
116: ## Inferred
117: 
118: - The omission is in physics coverage, not merely view projection: all stored `delta_from_default` cardinalities were measured, and none supplies a joint outcome.
119: - Any attempt to sum, interpolate, or otherwise combine singleton outcomes to stand in for the missing joint outcome would erase precisely the interaction term C3 is meant to learn.
120: - The current 15-anchor C1/C2 prefix is internally tied to one physics digest, but C3 cannot be made to share it without changing authenticated physics bytes. Therefore the runner must continue to fail closed on this corpus.
